      "writtenOn": "2022-02-02T14:34:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This logic seems unsafe?\n\n unique_ptr\u003cVarDecl\u003e a \u003d something;\n auto b \u003d a-\u003eclone();\n a \u003d nullptr;\n // b points to dead Variable?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 31,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "ac95531f1b2f28c0f74e182f1446ec76855064c9",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "58d0b42f_debfd934",
        "filename": "src/sksl/ir/SkSLVarDeclarations.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 5640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-02T15:26:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That would make b point to a variable that\u0027s dead in the \"will be removed if you run the optimizer\" sense, but not dead in the \"the memory it occupied has been freed\" sense (though obviously this will change if you run the optimizer).\n\nCloning a VarDeclaration without cloning the Variable it points to is inherently a weird, unsafe thing to be doing in the first place. The only reason we ever even do it is for this clone test, where we don\u0027t need to worry about tricky things like the original going away first and then the optimizer running.\n\nSince I don\u0027t see any reasonable way to simply clone() a VarDeclaration (you could also clone the Variable, but then you\u0027d also have to have some way to know which copy any subsequently-cloned variable references should point to...), logically we should just disallow cloning VarDeclarations altogether, but then we can\u0027t make the clone test work.\n\nWhat do you think about locking this stuff behind debug, and then just abort() if VarDeclaration.clone() is ever called in release? Or do you have a better suggestion?",
      "parentUuid": "fb442575_82fda9ba",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 31,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "ac95531f1b2f28c0f74e182f1446ec76855064c9",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "35bc0ee0_e4e8f2b7",
        "filename": "src/sksl/ir/SkSLVarDeclarations.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-02T16:43:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I agree, due to the bidirectional relationship between variables and vardecls, this is an inherently weird/semi-unsafe thing to do.\n\n(In my example code, after thinking it over, I think `b` would point to a Variable that is still allocated but is no longer associated to any VarDecl. The Variable would lose its initialValue() since that is tied to the VarDeclaration. Anything which relied on initialValue--the ConstantFolder is the biggest one--would stop working normally.)\n\nI\u0027m not sure I understand why cloning the Variable is off the table. The inliner uses `Variable::MakeScratchVariable` to make Variable clones and it seems to get away with it. But I\u0027m willing to believe it can\u0027t be done, since this stuff is inherently touchy.",
      "parentUuid": "58d0b42f_debfd934",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 31,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "ac95531f1b2f28c0f74e182f1446ec76855064c9",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "500fd72e_eeeccc59",
        "filename": "src/sksl/ir/SkSLVarDeclarations.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 48,
      "author": {
        "id": 5640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-02T20:19:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Cloning the variable certainly isn\u0027t impossible - but then you need to track which variable clones correspond to which variables so that you can rewrite the VariableReferences as needed, which means you also need some kind of overall context for the clone operation so that you know where to find this mapping. Inliner presumably does all of that internally and isn\u0027t just calling clone() without performing this sort of fixup.\n\nSo yes, you could make it work... but since this operation only really exists to serve this test, and the test doesn\u0027t need the variables to be cloned, it seems like an awful lot of work and complexity for no real benefit. Just having it fail in release (with, naturally, a comment thoroughly explaining the issue) seems to me like an adequate solution for now - if in the future we actually run into a need for this, we can do the required work then.",
      "parentUuid": "35bc0ee0_e4e8f2b7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 31,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 48,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "ac95531f1b2f28c0f74e182f1446ec76855064c9",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
